WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A diaper's polymer and pulp cost pennies, the one on the shelf costs several times the materials inside. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 74. A mid-size disposable diaper is a few dozen grams of sodium polyacrolate, fluff pulp, polypropylene, and a film of polyethylene, plus a pinch of elastic and adhesive. Those inputs, bought as commodities, add up to a handful of cents. The same diaper, taped into a printed pattern,

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:49.240
packaged on a grocery shelf, commonly sells for several times that material value, and the world's parents buy tens of billions of them a year. The prize is not a new miracle polymer. It is the gap between a cheap sponge and a branded single-use garment that we treat as if the two were the same object.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:01:16.780
Once the atoms and the isle are both in view, the rest of the story is architectural and logistical, not chemical. The modern disposable diaper is a high-speed converting product wearing a consumer brand costume. A continuous web of hydrophilic polypropylene nonwoven becomes the top sheet. An acquisition layer spreads the gush. A core of fluff pulp mixed with sodium polyacrolate granules takes and locks the liquid.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:36.820
In newer ultra-thin designs, that core is a mostly polymer laminate with almost no pulp at all. A polyethylene film often laminated to a cloth-like polypropylene back sheet keeps the outside dry. Standing leg gathers of hydrophobic nonwoven, threaded with elastic form the gasket.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:02:00.880
Waste elastics, ear panels, and a hook and loop or adhesive tape close the garment. Every one of those layers is well understood. The lines that assemble them are among the faster converting machines on earth, commonly running well above a thousand diapers a minute once they are up. That speed is why a factory gate diaper can exist at all as a mass market object rather than a hospital specialty.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:12.840
The price parents see is not set at that factory gate. It is set in a stack that grew up around a product whose job from the 1960s onward was to replace a wash cycle and a pin.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:30.020
Proctor and Gamble's Pampers and Kimberly Clark's Huggies taught a generation that the disposable was the default. Those companies then spent decades seeding maternity wards, buying pediatrician goodwill, and advertising on a scale that makes the purd diaper brand tax look small.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:51.280
And the annual brand tax look enormous. Grocery and mass retail learn to treat the aisle as a traffic engine and a slotting fee engine at the same time. A bulky, high-turn category still extracts a full retail margin, promotional funding, and a penalty for any stockkeeping unit or SKU that does not earn its facing.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:03:06.660
Because a diaper is mostly loft, a truck fills by volume long before it fills by weight. Freight and warehouse cube become a permanent tax on every unit that moves from a regional plant to a distribution center to a storeroom to a cart.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:18.580
Add the SKU tree, sizes one through six or seven, plus overnight, sensitive, swim, and the pull on training pant, and the chain is carrying compressed air in a dozen shapes.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:31.840
Each of those shapes carries its own forecast error and its own change over scrap. Inside the industry, this price feels normal because the reference class is other branded consumables, not the bill of materials.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:40.780
A name brand mid-sized tape diaper often lands somewhere around a quarter to forty cents each once you divide the box price by the count.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:50.220
Warehouse club and store brand lines frequently sit in the mid teens to mid-twenties of cents. Boutique or so-called natural lines run higher still.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:56.780
A child who has changed six to ten times a day will go through a few thousand diapers before potty training.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.660
So the household number that sticks in the mind is one or two thousand dollars, not twenty five cents.

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:08.760
That household number is what the brands are really selling, a year of dry sheets and no laundry.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.060
Cloth never disappeared, but it lost the mass market on labor and leak anxiety.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:28.860
Every subsequent design choice, more sodium polyacrylate, thinner cores, wetness stripes, lotioned top sheets, was an analogy move on top of a product whose architecture was already frozen as, throw the whole garment away.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:38.020
The converting industry optimized the frozen architecture brilliantly. It did not ask whether the garment and the sponge had to be the same disposable object.

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:47.700
That is the analogy world. Now look at the same object as atoms instead of a branded garment. Wave a magic wand and the atoms rearrange for free.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:55.460
What remains is the commodity value of the stuff, the theoretical floor, the number this show calls the magic wand number.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:04.420
A typical mid-size disposable often weighs somewhere in the mid-twenties to high thirties of grams, most of it the core and the non-woven's.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:11.280
Modern cores shifted from the early 1980s onward toward more super absorbent polymer and less fluff.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:18.100
Super absorbent polymer, or SAP, is the sodium polyacrylate that does the actual locking.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:28.160
A current mid-size tape diaper will commonly carry something like 10 grams, give or take a few, of that polymer, and a similar or smaller mass of fluff pulp.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:40.140
The ultra thin laminates lean even harder on the polymer. Hygiene-grade sodium polyacrylate has, across recent acrylic acid cycles, typically traded at a few dollars per kilogram.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:46.720
A working band of roughly two to four dollars a kilogram is fair, if you do not pretend to a spot quote.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:55.480
Walk the arithmetic out loud. 10 grams is one hundredth of a kilogram. 10 grams at three dollars a kilogram is three cents.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:06:03.880
That is the sponge's main actor, priced as a commodity. Fluff pulp is a specialized craft cellulose made to be fiberized.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:11.940
It often sits somewhere around one to two dollars a kilogram. 8 to 12 grams at that price lands in the neighborhood of one to two cents.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:21.180
The polypropylene in the top sheet, the back sheet, monwoven, and the cuffs, plus the polyethylene film, is a handful of grams of resins.

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:26.920
In ordinary commodity markets, those resins have often lived near one to two dollars a kilogram.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:38.260
A handful of grams at that price is about a penny of polymer. Elastane threads, hot melt adhesives, and a hook and loop tab are tiny masses at higher value per kilo.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.680
A couple of cents for the lot is a reasonable allowance rather than a measurement.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:53.440
Add those lines, three cents of super absorbent polymer, one to two cents of pulp, about a penny of film and nonwoven, and a couple of cents for the elastics and glue.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:07:01.140
You land on a rough magic wand estimate of about five to ten cents of commodity material in a typical mid-sized tape diaper.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:09.540
Push the wand all the way down to crude oil, salt, air, and a pine log, and the atomic floor is lower still, a few cents.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.400
Because you are looking at a 30 gram object made of hydrocarbons, cellulose, and a little sodium.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:24.780
Now the ratio. This show calls that ratio the idiot index. How many times more the finished thing costs than the raw materials inside it?

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:34.720
And only as arithmetic you can redo yourself. Take a mid-range name brand shelf price of roughly 30 cents and a material floor of, say, seven cents.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:40.260
30 divided by seven is a little over four. The idiot index is around four.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:49.780
Take a premium overnight that prices closer to 40 cents, or a small convenience store pack that is worse, against a stingy five cent material estimate.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:54.860
40 divided by five is eight. The ratio climbs towards six to eight.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:04.240
Take a warehouse club store brand at something like 18 cents against eight cents of materials. 18 divided by eight is a little over two.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:12.140
The index compresses toward two. None of those are audited bills of materials. They are envelopes on the back of an envelope.

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.680
And they should be read as a cluster, not a single sacred number. The cluster is consistent.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:27.560
Compared with the rocket engine stories this show tells, the cluster is modest. The finished diaper costs a small handful of times with the stuff inside it is worth.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:36.220
Not 50 times, and not one and a half. A moderate index on a tens of billions of dollars category is still a large pile of money.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:41.040
It is also a clue that the waste is not mostly hiding in a sloppy factory.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:51.320
Where the gap actually lives is easier to see if you stop treating the diaper as one cost and split the object into a sponge, a gasket, and a commercial stack.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:09:01.480
The sponge is already close to its floor. Sodium polyacrylate does the thermodynamic work. It can take 100 to 300 times its weight into still water.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:11.420
It can take only a few dozen times its weight in urine. The sodium and other ions in pee compete with the polymer's own carboxylate groups and collapse the swelling.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.500
That saline penalty is physics, not a vendor markup.

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:21.780
It is why a night diaper still needs a noticeable mass of super absorbent polymer rather than a pinch.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:29.080
Fluff pulp, where it remains, is there as a cheap distribution matrix and a cheap way to give the course of integrity.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:36.660
The ultra thin SAP laminates that have been rolling out for years are already the first principles answer to a simple question.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:41.960
Why are we paying to ship cellulose that does not lock liquid as well as the polymer does?

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:51.720
Converting the sponge into a shaped, channeled, tissue-wrapped core on a machine that also applies stretch, cuts ears, and folds a garment adds real cost.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:10:02.440
Depreciation on a line that costs many millions of dollars, plus energy, labor, and the scrap that appears every time a web breaks or a size change over runs out.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:15.400
That converting step can plausibly add several cents. It is not nothing. It is also not the majority of the gap to a 30 cent shelf price, because the industry has had 40 years to make those lines fast.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.760
The gasket is the next slice, and it is the slice most people do not price separately.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:30.320
Leg cuffs, waistband, stretch ears, and fasteners are there because a pad on a baby is useless if urine runs out the leg hole at 3 in the morning.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:38.860
Those components are a modest share of the mass, and a disproportionate share of the converting difficulty, the defect rate, and the design constraint.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.400
They also force the entire object to be thrown away.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:48.080
You cannot rinse a hook and loop ear and reuse it if it is glued to a urine soaked back sheet.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:58.120
So every change discards not only the spent super absorbent polymer, but a complete wearable that was expensive to assemble, and that had plenty of mechanical life left.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:07.860
That is an architectural choice, not a material one. It is the choice that keeps the idiot index from collapsing toward 2 even when the polymer is cheap.

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:19.700
The rest of the gap is commercial and geometric, brand advertising, hospital seating, and that this is what a good parent buys campaign, do not show up as a line on the bill of materials.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:29.460
They are recovered in the name brand premium over store brand, often a gap of 10 to 20 cents a unit, which is larger than the entire magic wand floor.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.940
Retailer margin, slotting, and trade promotions sit on top of that.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:44.560
This prices are inflated so that coupons and warehouse club discounts can be carved back out, and the bulky pack still has to justify its facing against formula and wipes.

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:49.400
Then there is cube, a compressed jumbo pack is still mostly air.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:57.940
Regional distribution of a low density consumable means you are paying to haul loft, to store loft, and to handle loft at every dock.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:04.340
Fright per kilogram looks absurdly high until you remember that the kilogram is not what filled the trailer.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.140
SKU proliferation multiplies the same tax.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:17.020
Overnight versus daytime, newborn cut versus size 4, a lotioned top sheet versus a plain one, each with its own safety stock.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:24.860
Working capital follows the cube. None of this is a scandal unique to diapers. It is how branded consumables work.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:30.940
But it is why a product whose materials are nickels becomes a product whose shelf price is quarters.

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:38.620
And it is why the people inside the category can recite that is just what diapers cost, without ever adding up the polymer.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.460
Two objections deserve to be set out loud.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.920
First, manufacturing really is already good.

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:53.560
Anyone who walks a modern diaper line and then talks as if the opportunity were invent a cheaper way to glue nonwoven has not looked.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:56.840
The remaining converting waste is real and finite.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:06.240
Changeovers, start up scrap, over build of super absorbent polymer for the 99th percentile wetter, so the median diaper carries unused polymer.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:12.480
That is nickels and fractions of nickels, worth chasing at this volume, not a rewrite of the industry.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.400
Second, cloth is not a free lunch.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:24.400
A washable cotton or microfiber system shifts the cost into water, heat, detergent, drier cycles or line time, and parental labor.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:29.040
The leak performance of most cloth systems is why the mass market left.

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:36.400
A first principle's attack that simply says, wash it, has not priced the scarce resource, which is not cellulose.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:41.080
It is a dry bed at three in the morning and a parent who still has to go to work.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:46.760
A moderate index on a frozen architecture means the price is not cheaper glue.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:50.060
The first move is to stop throwing away the gasket.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:14:05.280
Split the product into a durable chassis, the belt, the leg cuffs, the fasteners, the thing that actually has to fit a wriggling body, and a disposable core that is nothing but the acquisition layer, the SAP matrix, and a thin back sheet.

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:09.600
You wash or wipe the chassis, you throw away only the spent sponge.

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:15.180
Hybrids of this shape already exist in the cloth adjacent and some institutional markets.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:23.780
They have not won the mass market because the chassis has never been as fast as forgiving and as obviously clean as a tape diaper in the dark.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:28.720
That is the design problem worth solving, not a new brand of the old garment.

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:33.780
For the move to work, the insert has to hit nighttime capacity without shifting.

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:40.980
The chassis has to survive hundreds of uses without the elastics dying, and the whole ritual has to be one handed.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:48.040
If those three are true, most of the converting cost and most of the discarded nonwoven leave the disposable unit.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:52.860
The idiot index on the thrown away piece can fall toward the material floor.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:58.860
What you save is the gasket you were manufacturing a few thousand times per child and using once.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:02.100
The second move is to kill the freight of air.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:07.620
Dipers are a regional manufacturing product pretending to be a national brand product.

00:15:08.060 --> 00:15:18.460
A from scratch network would put simpler converting, especially of cores and inserts, which are flatter and more compressible than finished tape diapers, closer to the population.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:23.980
Or it would redesign the pack itself so that a trailer carries mass instead of loft.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:28.920
Store brands already captured a large share of the brand tax opportunity.

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:36.600
Kirkland and the grocer labels are the existence proof that parents will accept a lower prestige mark when the leak rate is fine.

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:39.420
What they did not capture is the cube.

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:48.020
A regional insert plant feeding a reusable chassis, or even a regional full diaper plant that refuses the national distribution center hop,

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.360
is a logistics redesign, not a chemistry redesign.

00:15:51.360 --> 00:16:01.920
It needs scale in a metro area, a retailer or subscription channel that will take a shorter supply chain, and a skew list short enough that changeovers do not eat the freight win.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:08.260
The save is sense per unit on a few billion units, which is the only way sense become a company.

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:12.480
The third move is to stop over building the median core for the tail.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:21.720
Channel cores and sap laminates are already the industry's own first principles work on this, and these should be treated as the baseline, not the innovation.

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:25.560
What is still sitting on the table is honest segmentation.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:37.220
A daytime insert sized for a few hours, a nighttime insert that carries the extra polymer, and an end to shipping a night core in every change because the aisle is easier to plan that way.

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:52.580
For that to be true at scale, the parent has to be able to grab the right pad half asleep, which means the packaging and the chassis interface have to make the choice obvious, and the brand has to accept that a smaller daytime pivot is not a confession of cheapness.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:17:01.720
Adult incontinence, which uses the same construction and is growing with age, is the quieter and possibly larger canvas for all three moves.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:08.560
Less three in the morning panic, more willingness to handle a two-piece system, and a buyer who can do arithmetic.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:23.460
The hard parts are not mystical, skin microclimate, humidity, pH, time next to urine, is a real medical constraint, and a redesign that raises rash rates is a failed redesign no matter what the idiot index does.

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:32.760
Leak forgiveness is the feature people actually buy, a chassis plus insert that loses on the first week of real babies will not get a second week.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:35.760
Capital lock-in is boring and decisive.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:46.080
The installed base of full garment lines wants to keep making full garments, and a new architecture needs either a new line or a customer large enough to strand the old ones.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:55.000
Trust is slower than polymer chemistry, and the moderate index means there is no 50 times jackpot hiding in a better acrylic acid contract.

00:17:55.540 --> 00:18:00.640
The prize is architectural and logistical, collected in nickels, or it is not collected.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.840
Sodium polyacrylate already did the chemistry.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:10.860
What we still buy is a complete wearable, manufactured at enormous speed, so that a sponge can be thrown away.

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:15.000
After the polymer is counted, the wearable is most of the bill.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:19.080
Cube is a physical tax that no brand campaign can repeal.

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:28.740
Once you decide to ship loft across a continent, you have chosen a floor well above the atoms, and the only way down is a denser object or a shorter haul.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:34.140
A moderate idiot index on tens of billions of units is not a scandal in the factory.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:41.960
It is a signal that the architecture and the trailer are doing the expensive work, and that another scented top sheet will not move the ratio.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:48.260
The first concrete signal that someone is actually trying this would not be another allocented top sheet.

00:18:48.780 --> 00:19:01.880
It would be a chassis and insert, sold at store brand money rather than boutique money, that survives a real night in a mainstream size, or a regional plant publishing a pack that finally weighs what the trailer says it should.

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:09.380
Who is close enough to the converting line, and tired enough of hauling air to build that instead of another printed bag?

